= Practice Questions (Exam Pattern)

Ten questions in the CUET UG style. Attempt all of them — the paper gives $+5$ for each correct answer and takes nothing away for a wrong one.

#mcq(1, [Which sugar is NOT a reducing sugar?], (
  [glucose], [maltose], [lactose], [sucrose],
))

#mcq(2, [The glycosidic linkage in sucrose connects:], (
  [glucose C1 and glucose C4], [glucose C1 and fructose C2], [galactose and glucose], [two fructose units],
))

#mcq(3, [Cellulose is a polymer of β-glucose joined by:], (
  [α-1,4 linkages], [β-1,4 linkages], [α-1,6 linkages], [β-1,2 linkages],
))

#mcq(4, [The D-configuration of glucose refers to:], (
  [its rotation of light to the right], [the OH on the penultimate carbon being on the right], [its D-shaped ring], [its density],
))

#mcq(5, [Glucose's failure to give an instant Schiff's test shows that:], (
  [it is not an aldehyde at all], [most molecules exist as the hemiacetal ring, with little free CHO], [it is a ketose], [it lacks OH groups],
))

#mcq(6, [Which amino acid is essential in the human diet?], (
  [glycine], [alanine], [lysine], [aspartic acid],
))

#mcq(7, [Denaturation of a protein destroys:], (
  [the primary structure], [the peptide bonds], [the secondary and tertiary structure], [all covalent bonds],
))

#mcq(8, [The base NOT present in DNA is:], (
  [adenine], [guanine], [uracil], [cytosine],
))

#mcq(9, [Vitamin deficiency matching — which pair is correct?], (
  [Vitamin C — beriberi], [Vitamin B₁ — scurvy], [Vitamin D — rickets], [Vitamin K — night blindness],
))

#mcq(10, [Insulin deficiency causes:], (
  [goitre], [rickets], [diabetes mellitus], [pernicious anaemia],
))

= Detailed Solutions

#solution(1, [Reducing sugars carry a free anomeric carbon. Sucrose's glycosidic bond ties up BOTH anomeric carbons (glucose C1 to fructose C2) — no free CHO/C=O, no reduction. Every other option reduces Tollen's/Fehling's.], answer: [D])

#solution(2, [Sucrose links the anomeric carbons of its two parents: glucose C1 (α) to fructose C2 (β) — the α-1,2 glycosidic bond. Maltose is α-1,4 of two glucoses; lactose joins galactose to glucose.], answer: [B])

#solution(3, [Cellulose's β-1,4 links build straight, flat chains that H-bond into fibres — the reason paper and cotton hold shape, and why human enzymes cannot digest them. Starch uses α-links we CAN digest.], answer: [B])

#solution(4, [D/L reads the penultimate carbon's OH in the Fischer projection — right = D. Optical rotation is an independent measurement: D-glucose is (+) but D-fructose is (−). Configuration ≠ rotation sign.], answer: [B])

#solution(5, [The open-chain aldehyde exists only fleetingly — nearly all glucose sits as the pyranose hemiacetal ring. Schiff's reagent (and 2,4-DNP/bisulphite) reacts too slowly to catch it.], answer: [B])

#solution(6, [The ten essential amino acids must come from food — lysine is the classic example. Glycine, alanine and aspartic acid are non-essential: the body builds them itself.], answer: [C])

#solution(7, [Denaturation unwinds the H-bonded helices and 3-D folds (secondary/tertiary) but never touches the covalent peptide-bonded sequence. Boiled egg: unfolded, still a chain of amino acids.], answer: [C])

#solution(8, [DNA's alphabet is A, T, G, C — uracil belongs to RNA, replacing thymine (with ribose as the sugar instead of deoxyribose).], answer: [C])

#solution(9, [Vitamin D builds bones — its shortage bends them (rickets). The mislabelled pairs: beriberi is B₁, scurvy is C, night blindness is A.], answer: [C])

#solution(10, [Insulin manages blood glucose; its shortage is diabetes mellitus. Goitre tracks thyroxine/iodine; pernicious anaemia tracks B₁₂.], answer: [C])

